# Planning chain hand-off (grill → PRD → slices → execution)

> Always-on rule. Cursor twin of [`.claude/rules/planning-chain-handoff.md`](../../.claude/rules/planning-chain-handoff.md) — keep them in sync when you edit either.
> Canon for how a **planning** session ends.
> Execution slices are governed by [`auto-merge-policy.mdc`](./auto-merge-policy.mdc) and
> [`hitl-afk-slices.mdc`](./hitl-afk-slices.mdc), not by this file.

## The problem this fixes

The chain is four sessions — **grill → PRD → slices → execution** — and the CEO used to carry the
baton between them by hand: compose a prompt, remember which step comes next, remember which
document it reads, and remember whether the previous step's PR had merged yet.

That last one was the expensive part. A slice-planning prompt pasted against a PRD still sitting in
an open PR starts a session that plans from a document it cannot see, and nothing says so until the
work is already wrong.

**The CEO does not review planning documents.** They were in the room for the grill; re-reading the
write-up of a conversation they just had is not review, it is repetition.

## The rule

**A planning session closes its own loop.** It merges its own PR when green, and it prints — or
starts — the next session. The CEO's only job between planning steps is to say "go" if they want to.

### 1. Planning PRs auto-merge when green

A **planning PR** is one that ships only the artefacts of grill, PRD or slice planning:

- `docs/projects/*-prd.md`, `*-master.md`, `*-grill-*.md`, a PRD brief under `docs/**/plans/`
- the program registry row in `platform-migration-master.md` §9.2
- `scripts/ralph-master-registry.mjs` — the slice-id registration slice planning must do

All of these must hold, or it is not a planning PR and this section does not apply:

1. The diff touches **nothing else** — no `src/`, no `supabase/`, no `functions/`, no `packages/`
2. Every check reported and none objected — proven by `npm run mc:merge-verdict`, **never by eye**
3. The SESSION REPORT is in the PR body
4. The STATUS DASHBOARD is updated in the same PR

Then merge it. Do not ask. Do not leave it open "for review" — there is no review coming.

**Everything outside that list keeps its existing gate.** A planning PR that also changes a line of
code is not a planning PR; it goes back to [`auto-merge-policy.mdc`](./auto-merge-policy.mdc) and its
deny list. This carve-out buys convenience with **scope**, not with the gate.

### 2. Then hand off — derived, never hand-written

```bash
npm run mc:handoff -- <program>
```

It reads the master doc's `GRILL:` / `PRD:` / `SLICING:` dashboard lines, picks the first that is not
✅, and prints the exact prompt for that step. `--json` gives the same thing machine-readable.

**It refuses to hand off across an unmerged document.** If the doc the next session must read is not
on `origin/main`, it says so and exits 1 instead of printing a prompt that would fail. That refusal
is the whole point of the script; do not work around it by writing the prompt by hand.

Because the prompt is derived from the dashboard, the three planning skills do **not** each carry
their own copy of it. One source, one place to fix it.

### 3. Start the next session for the CEO where you can

If the session has the session-spawning MCP tools (Claude Code) or a new Cursor Cloud Agent, do not stop at printing:

```
mcp__Claude_Code_Remote__create_session({
  title: "<PROGRAM> — <next step>",
  prompt: "<the prompt from mc:handoff --json>",
  tags: ["planning-chain", "<program>"],
  source_url: "https://github.com/johndaryow/pp-workspace",
})
```

**`source_url` is not optional, and leaving it off is a silent failure.** A new session inherits the
parent's *environment* but not its repository: without this line the session starts with an empty
working directory, and the prompt it was handed — which opens with `npm run mc:status` — has no
`package.json` to run, no master doc to read and no rules or skills to load. It does not error at
launch; it wastes the first minutes of a session working out that it has nothing to work on.
Measured on LNP-1, 2026-08-18: the slice ran only because the agent recognised the empty directory
and attached the repo itself.

Add `source_revision` only to pin a non-default branch. The chain always wants the default branch,
so normally omit it.

Then give the CEO the session link in one line. **Ask before creating it** the first time in a
conversation — a session is a real, billable thing, and the CEO may want to run the step themselves,
later, or on a different machine. Once they say yes, keep going without asking again in that chat.

If the tools are absent, print the prompt in a fenced block the CEO can copy in one click, and say
which skill it runs.

### 4. Say where the chain now stands

Last line of the SESSION REPORT, always:

```
Next: <step> — <one plain sentence>. Prompt: `npm run mc:handoff -- <program>`
```

## What still stops for the CEO

This rule removes a chore, not a judgement. Unchanged:

- **A grill itself is a conversation.** It is HITL by definition and never auto-anything.
- **`CEO_GATE: explicit_ok_in_chat`** on any slice — planning does not pre-approve execution.
- **Open questions.** If a planning session ends with a real question, ask it and hand off anyway —
  the next step usually does not depend on the answer. Say plainly which one it is if it does.
- **Nothing merges red.** "Green" means the gate said ALLOW. Zero checks is a failure, never a pass.

## The execution twin

Execution slices are not governed by this file, but they now follow the same principle: a session that
queues or finishes work ends by printing the next prompt from `npm run mc:opener -- <program>`, never
by telling the CEO to type "Continue". Canon for that half:
[`agent-chat-session.mdc`](./agent-chat-session.mdc) → *Never offer "Continue"*.

